[FINAL-TEXT] I have all the evidence I need. The fault is fully localised.

**Summary of findings:**
- **Router4 eth4** (`link_r4_r5`, 192.168.15.2/24 → router5): **DOWN**
- **Router4 eth5** (`link_r4_r3`, 172.25.1.1/24 → router3): **DOWN**
- **Router4 routing table**: only one route — directly connected `192.168.200.0/24`; no routes to 172.16.10.0/24 or anywhere else
- **Router3 eth5** (172.25.1.2/24): **UP** — the peer end of the link is fine; the fault is on router4's side only
- **PC11 → router0 ping**: "Destination Net Unreachable" generated by router4 itself (192.168.200.1), confirming router4 drops the packets immediately

---

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
Both uplink interfaces on router4 have been administratively brought down — eth4 (192.168.15.2/24, the `link_r4_r5` segment to router5) and eth5 (172.25.1.1/24, the `link_r4_r3` segment to router3) are in Linux `state DOWN` with no `UP` flag. Router4 is the sole gateway for Location C (192.168.200.0/24), which hangs off its eth0 via switch4 → switch5/switch6.

Because eth4 and eth5 are down, ripd on router4 cannot form RIP adjacencies with either router3 or router5. As a result, router4's FIB contains only a single directly-connected route (192.168.200.0/24). When PC11–14 send packets destined for Location A (172.16.10.0/24 or 172.16.20.0/24), they reach router4's eth0 (their default gateway at 192.168.200.1), but router4 has no matching route and immediately returns ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" — confirmed by a live ping from PC11. Router3's eth5 (172.25.1.2/24, the peer end of the link_r4_r3 segment) is UP and healthy, confirming the fault is isolated to router4's side.

Root cause: both WAN-facing interfaces on router4 (eth4 and eth5) were administratively taken down, severing all RIP adjacencies and leaving router4 without any routes beyond its local subnet.

Corrective fix: on router4, run `ip link set eth4 up && ip link set eth5 up`. Once the links come up, ripd will re-establish adjacencies with router3 (via 172.25.1.x) and router5 (via 192.168.15.x), Location A routes will propagate into router4's table, and PC11–14 will regain full connectivity to Location A.
=== END ===